---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import BuildUpLink from "../../components/UnderTheHood/BuildUpLink.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
import IconOnlyDemo from "../../components/Blog/IconOnly.astro"
import IconOnly from "../../component-examples/button/IconOnly.astro"
---

<TechniquePost slug="button-icon-only-has">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      An icon-only button needs to be square: no inline padding, the same width
      as height, and a bigger icon. Most libraries ship a separate <code
        >IconButton</code
      > or an <code>iconOnly</code> prop for it, and every size, variant and state
      gets implemented twice.
    </p>
    <p>
      CSS couldn't style an element based on what's inside it, so the markup had
      to say it. Now the button can look at its own children.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>:has(&gt; svg:only-child)</code> matches a button whose only child element
        is an icon
      </li>
      <li>
        The minimum width becomes the button's height, so it's square at every
        size
      </li>
      <li>The icon scales with the height instead of the line</li>
      <li>
        <code>:where()</code> adds no specificity, so your overrides still win
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`.button {
  --icon: 0.7lh;
  --min-height: var(--ui-button-size);
  min-block-size: var(--min-height);
  padding-inline: 1.5ex;
}

.button:where(:has(> svg:only-child)) {
  --icon: calc(var(--min-height) * 0.6);
  min-inline-size: var(--min-height);
  padding-inline: 0;
}

.button > svg {
  max-block-size: var(--icon);
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Delete the label. Once the icon is the only child, the button turns
      square, and the label comes back as <code>aria-label</code>.
    </p>
    <Example showCode={false}>
      <IconOnlyDemo />
    </Example>
    <p>Every button here holds only an icon, so every one is square.</p>
    <Example column spacious showCode={false}>
      <IconOnly />
    </Example>
    <BuildUpLink />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Without <code>:has()</code>, the icon-only rules are ignored
      </li>
      <li>
        The button keeps its text padding and line-sized icon: wider than
        square, but it works
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="gotchas">Gotchas</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>:only-child</code> only counts elements. In <code
          >&lt;svg&gt; Download</code
        >, the text node doesn't count, so the button is treated as icon-only.
        Wrap the label in a <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>
      </li>
      <li>An icon-only button still needs an <code>aria-label</code></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
